<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coptic Orthodox Church</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Coptic Church</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Coptic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> is the main Christian church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, headed by the Pope of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. It rejected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a> in 451 and became separate from the imperial church, and most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> followed it until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> gradually spread after the Arab conquest. It also consecrated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Christian Nubian kingdoms such as Makuria, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coptic-orthodox-church.jpg" alt="Coptic Orthodox Church" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland Unger · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CairoAbbasiyaMarkEntrance.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to Coptic tradition the church was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-the-evangelist">Saint Mark</a> the Evangelist, a companion of the Apostles, around 42, and it regards itself as the subject of many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a> prophecies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catechetical-school-of-alexandria">Catechetical School of Alexandria</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> records as founded by Mark himself, is the oldest catechetical school in the world. It became a leading centre of the allegorical method of biblical interpretation and sought a rapprochement between Greek culture and the Christian faith.</p><p>In the 4th century an Alexandrian presbyter named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arius">Arius</a> began a dispute about the nature of Christ. Emperor Constantine I convened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">Council of Nicaea</a> in 325 after Pope Alexander I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> proposed a council, and it led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-creed">Nicene Creed</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a> in 451, disputes over the nature of Christ left the Oriental Orthodox churches in schism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Muslim invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> took place in 639. Bishop John of Nikiu, whose Chronicle survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geez">Ge'ez</a>, gives a hostile account of the conquest, though he says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> took none of the property of the churches. The population remained mainly Christian at first, but gradual conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> made Egypt a largely Muslim country by the end of the 12th century. Scholars point to the repression of Coptic revolts, Arab-Muslim immigration and conversion, and to higher Umayyad taxes on Christians, which weakened the church's economic base. The church moved its seat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in the 12th century.</p><p>The position of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copts">Copts</a> began to improve early in the 19th century under the Muhammad Ali dynasty, and Sa'id Pasha abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> tax in 1855. Pope Cyril IV founded the first modern Coptic schools in 1853, including the first Egyptian school for girls, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press. The Theological College of the School of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> was re-established in 1893.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The pope of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> heads the church, which follows the Coptic Rite. The language of its liturgy descends from Ancient Egyptian and uses the Coptic alphabet, derived from the Greek with added letters from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demotic-egyptian-script">Demotic</a>. Since Coptic was supplanted by Arabic after the Arab conquest, service books carry Arabic in parallel columns. Believers use a breviary called the Agpeya to pray seven times a day, facing east.</p><p>The church has long consecrated the archbishops of the Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church, whose first native head, Abuna Basilios, was crowned patriarch by Pope Cyril VI in 1959. The Eritrean church gained autocephaly in 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Coptic Orthodox Church” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the union with Nobadia, Makuria followed the Miaphysite <b>Coptic Church</b>, and its bishops, at sees such as Dongola, Faras, Qasr Ibrim and Sai, were consecrated by the Coptic Patriarch of Alexandria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Coptic, Egyptian written in Greek letters with several signs taken from Demotic, became the language of a new literature and is still used in the liturgy of the <b>Coptic Orthodox Church</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Coptic language, the last stage of Egyptian, survives in the liturgy of the <b>Coptic Church</b>, and some modern groups seek to revive the worship of the ancient gods.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It remains the liturgical language of the <b>Coptic Orthodox Church</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Its great authors were saints of the <b>Coptic Church</b>, Anthony the Great, Pachomius the Great and Shenoute, whose many sermons, treatises and homilies laid the basis of early Coptic literature and helped to standardise the language.</blockquote><a href="/language/coptic">Coptic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall of Kush, missionaries from Aksum and from Egypt competed in Nubia, and the <b>Coptic Church</b> established its authority.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nubians">Nubians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
